See \fBOptions\fP section\&.
.SS "\fRescapePath(path)\fP"
Returns the path with escaped special characters depending on the platform\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Posix:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR*?|(){}[]\fP;
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR!\fP at the beginning of line;
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR@+!\fP before the opening parenthesis;
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR\\\\\fP before non-special characters;
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Windows:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR(){}[]\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR!\fP at the beginning of line;
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fR@+!\fP before the opening parenthesis;
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Characters like \fR*?|\fP cannot be used in the path (\fRwindows_naming_conventions\fP), so they will not be escaped;
.PP

.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.escapePath('!abc');
// \\\\!abc
fg\&.escapePath('[OpenSource] mrmlnc – fast\-glob (Deluxe Edition) 2014') + '/*\&.flac'
// \\\\[OpenSource\\\\] mrmlnc – fast\-glob \\\\(Deluxe Edition\\\\) 2014/*\&.flac

fg\&.posix\&.escapePath('C:\\\\Program Files (x86)\\\\**\\\\*');
// C:\\\\\\\\Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)\\\\*\\\\*\\\\*
fg\&.win32\&.escapePath('C:\\\\Program Files (x86)\\\\**\\\\*');
// Windows: C:\\\\Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)\\\\**\\\\*
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRconvertPathToPattern(path)\fP"
Converts a path to a pattern depending on the platform, including special character escaping\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Posix\&. Works similarly to the \fRfg\&.posix\&.escapePath\fP method\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Windows\&. Works similarly to the \fRfg\&.win32\&.escapePath\fP method, additionally converting backslashes to forward slashes in cases where they are not escape characters (\fR!()+@{}[]\fP)\&.
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.convertPathToPattern('[OpenSource] mrmlnc – fast\-glob (Deluxe Edition) 2014') + '/*\&.flac';
// \\\\[OpenSource\\\\] mrmlnc – fast\-glob \\\\(Deluxe Edition\\\\) 2014/*\&.flac

fg\&.convertPathToPattern('C:/Program Files (x86)/**/*');
// Posix: C:/Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)/\\\\*\\\\*/\\\\*
// Windows: C:/Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)/**/*

fg\&.convertPathToPattern('C:\\\\Program Files (x86)\\\\**\\\\*');
// Posix: C:\\\\\\\\Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)\\\\*\\\\*\\\\*
// Windows: C:/Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)/**/*

fg\&.posix\&.convertPathToPattern('\\\\\\\\?\\\\c:\\\\Program Files (x86)') + '/**/*';
// Posix: \\\\\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\c:\\\\\\\\Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)/**/* (broken pattern)
fg\&.win32\&.convertPathToPattern('\\\\\\\\?\\\\c:\\\\Program Files (x86)') + '/**/*';
// Windows: //?/c:/Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)/**/*
.fi
.PP
.SH "Options"
.PP
.SS "Common options"
.SS "concurrency"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRnumber\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRos\&.cpus()\&.length\fP
.PP

.PP
Specifies the maximum number of concurrent requests from a reader to read directories\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:book: The higher the number, the higher the performance and load on the file system\&. If you want to read in quiet mode, set the value to a comfortable number or \fR1\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fBMore details\fP
.PP
.PP
.RS 4


.PP

.PP
In Node, there are \fRtwo types of threads\fP: Event Loop (code) and a Thread Pool (fs, dns, …)\&. The thread pool size controlled by the \fRUV_THREADPOOL_SIZE\fP environment variable\&. Its default size is 4 (\fRdocumentation\fP)\&. The pool is one for all tasks within a single Node process\&.

.PP
Any code can make 4 real concurrent accesses to the file system\&. The rest of the FS requests will wait in the queue\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:book: Each new instance of FG in the same Node process will use the same Thread pool\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
But this package also has the \fRconcurrency\fP option\&. This option allows you to control the number of concurrent accesses to the FS at the package level\&. By default, this package has a value equal to the number of cores available for the current Node process\&. This allows you to set a value smaller than the pool size (\fRconcurrency: 1\fP) or, conversely, to prepare tasks for the pool queue more quickly (\fRconcurrency: Number\&.POSITIVE_INFINITY\fP)\&.

.PP
So, in fact, this package can \fBonly make 4 concurrent requests to the FS\fP\&. You can increase this value by using an environment variable (\fRUV_THREADPOOL_SIZE\fP), but in practice this does not give a multiple advantage\&.

.PP
.RE
.PP
.SS "cwd"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRstring\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRprocess\&.cwd()\fP
.PP

.PP
The current working directory in which to search\&.
.SS "deep"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRnumber\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRInfinity\fP
.PP

.PP
Specifies the maximum depth of a read directory relative to the start directory\&.

.PP
For example, you have the following tree:

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
└── one/            // 1
    └── two/        // 2
        └── file\&.js // 3
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
// With base directory
fg\&.sync('dir/**', { onlyFiles: false, deep: 1 }); // ['dir/one']
fg\&.sync('dir/**', { onlyFiles: false, deep: 2 }); // ['dir/one', 'dir/one/two']

// With cwd option
fg\&.sync('**', { onlyFiles: false, cwd: 'dir', deep: 1 }); // ['one']
fg\&.sync('**', { onlyFiles: false, cwd: 'dir', deep: 2 }); // ['one', 'one/two']
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: If you specify a pattern with some base directory, this directory will not participate in the calculation of the depth of the found directories\&. Think of it as a \fB`cwd`\fP option\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "followSymbolicLinks"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Indicates whether to traverse descendants of symbolic link directories when expanding \fR**\fP patterns\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:book: Note that this option does not affect the base directory of the pattern\&. For example, if \fR\&./a\fP is a symlink to directory \fR\&./b\fP and you specified \fR['\&./a**', '\&./b/**']\fP patterns, then directory \fR\&./a\fP will still be read\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: If the \fB`stats`\fP option is specified, the information about the symbolic link (\fRfs\&.lstat\fP) will be replaced with information about the entry (\fRfs\&.stat\fP) behind it\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "fs"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRFileSystemAdapter\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfs\&.*\fP
.PP

.PP
Custom implementation of methods for working with the file system\&. Supports objects with enumerable properties only\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
export interface FileSystemAdapter {
    lstat?: typeof fs\&.lstat;
    stat?: typeof fs\&.stat;
    lstatSync?: typeof fs\&.lstatSync;
    statSync?: typeof fs\&.statSync;
    readdir?: typeof fs\&.readdir;
    readdirSync?: typeof fs\&.readdirSync;
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "ignore"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRstring[]\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fR[]\fP
.PP

.PP
An array of glob patterns to exclude matches\&. This is an alternative way to use negative patterns\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
├── package\-lock\&.json
└── package\&.json
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync(['*\&.json', '!package\-lock\&.json']);            // ['package\&.json']
fg\&.sync('*\&.json', { ignore: ['package\-lock\&.json'] }); // ['package\&.json']
.fi
.PP
.SS "suppressErrors"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
By default this package suppress only \fRENOENT\fP errors\&. Set to \fRtrue\fP to suppress any error\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:book: Can be useful when the directory has entries with a special level of access\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "throwErrorOnBrokenSymbolicLink"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Throw an error when symbolic link is broken if \fRtrue\fP or safely return \fRlstat\fP call if \fRfalse\fP\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:book: This option has no effect on errors when reading the symbolic link directory\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "Output control"
.SS "absolute"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Return the absolute path for entries\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*\&.js', { absolute: false }); // ['index\&.js']
fg\&.sync('*\&.js', { absolute: true });  // ['/home/user/index\&.js']
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: This option is required if you want to use negative patterns with absolute path, for example, \fR!${__dirname}/*\&.js\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "markDirectories"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Mark the directory path with the final slash\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*', { onlyFiles: false, markDirectories: false }); // ['index\&.js', 'controllers']
fg\&.sync('*', { onlyFiles: false, markDirectories: true });  // ['index\&.js', 'controllers/']
.fi
.PP
.SS "objectMode"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Returns objects (instead of strings) describing entries\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*', { objectMode: false }); // ['src/index\&.js']
fg\&.sync('*', { objectMode: true });  // [{ name: 'index\&.js', path: 'src/index\&.js', dirent: <fs\&.Dirent> }]
.fi
.PP

.PP
The object has the following fields:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
name (\fRstring\fP) — the last part of the path (basename)
.IP "\(bu" 2
path (\fRstring\fP) — full path relative to the pattern base directory
.IP "\(bu" 2
dirent (\fR\fRfs\&.Dirent\fP\fP) — instance of \fRfs\&.Dirent\fP
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: An object is an internal representation of entry, so getting it does not affect performance\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "onlyDirectories"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Return only directories\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*', { onlyDirectories: false }); // ['index\&.js', 'src']
fg\&.sync('*', { onlyDirectories: true });  // ['src']
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: If \fRtrue\fP, the \fB`onlyFiles`\fP option is automatically \fRfalse\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "onlyFiles"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Return only files\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*', { onlyFiles: false }); // ['index\&.js', 'src']
fg\&.sync('*', { onlyFiles: true });  // ['index\&.js']
.fi
.PP
.SS "stats"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Enables an \fBobject mode\fP with an additional field:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
stats (\fR\fRfs\&.Stats\fP\fP) — instance of \fRfs\&.Stats\fP
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*', { stats: false }); // ['src/index\&.js']
fg\&.sync('*', { stats: true });  // [{ name: 'index\&.js', path: 'src/index\&.js', dirent: <fs\&.Dirent>, stats: <fs\&.Stats> }]
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: Returns \fRfs\&.stat\fP instead of \fRfs\&.lstat\fP for symbolic links when the \fB`followSymbolicLinks`\fP option is specified\&.

.PP
:warning: Unlike \fBobject mode\fP this mode requires additional calls to the file system\&. On average, this mode is slower at least twice\&. See \fBold and modern mode\fP for more details\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "unique"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Ensures that the returned entries are unique\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync(['*\&.json', 'package\&.json'], { unique: false }); // ['package\&.json', 'package\&.json']
fg\&.sync(['*\&.json', 'package\&.json'], { unique: true });  // ['package\&.json']
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fRtrue\fP and similar entries are found, the result is the first found\&.
.SS "Matching control"
.SS "braceExpansion"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Enables Bash-like brace expansion\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:1234: \fRSyntax description\fP or more \fRdetailed description\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
├── abd
├── acd
└── a{b,c}d
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('a{b,c}d', { braceExpansion: false }); // ['a{b,c}d']
fg\&.sync('a{b,c}d', { braceExpansion: true });  // ['abd', 'acd']
.fi
.PP
.SS "caseSensitiveMatch"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Enables a \fRcase-sensitive\fP mode for matching files\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
├── file\&.txt
└── File\&.txt
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('file\&.txt', { caseSensitiveMatch: false }); // ['file\&.txt', 'File\&.txt']
fg\&.sync('file\&.txt', { caseSensitiveMatch: true });  // ['file\&.txt']
.fi
.PP
.SS "dot"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
Allow patterns to match entries that begin with a period (\fR\&.\fP)\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:book: Note that an explicit dot in a portion of the pattern will always match dot files\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
├── \&.editorconfig
└── package\&.json
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*', { dot: false }); // ['package\&.json']
fg\&.sync('*', { dot: true });  // ['\&.editorconfig', 'package\&.json']
.fi
.PP
.SS "extglob"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Enables Bash-like \fRextglob\fP functionality\&.

.PP
.RS 4
:1234: \fRSyntax description\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
├── README\&.md
└── package\&.json
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*\&.+(json|md)', { extglob: false }); // []
fg\&.sync('*\&.+(json|md)', { extglob: true });  // ['README\&.md', 'package\&.json']
.fi
.PP
.SS "globstar"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRtrue\fP
.PP

.PP
Enables recursively repeats a pattern containing \fR**\fP\&. If \fRfalse\fP, \fR**\fP behaves exactly like \fR*\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
└── a
    └── b
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('**', { onlyFiles: false, globstar: false }); // ['a']
fg\&.sync('**', { onlyFiles: false, globstar: true });  // ['a', 'a/b']
.fi
.PP
.SS "baseNameMatch"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: \fRboolean\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Default: \fRfalse\fP
.PP

.PP
If set to \fRtrue\fP, then patterns without slashes will be matched against the basename of the path if it contains slashes\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
└── one/
    └── file\&.md
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync('*\&.md', { baseNameMatch: false }); // []
fg\&.sync('*\&.md', { baseNameMatch: true });  // ['one/file\&.md']
.fi
.PP
.SH "FAQ"
.PP
.SH "What is a static or dynamic pattern?"
.PP
All patterns can be divided into two types:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBstatic\fP\&. A pattern is considered static if it can be used to get an entry on the file system without using matching mechanisms\&. For example, the \fRfile\&.js\fP pattern is a static pattern because we can just verify that it exists on the file system\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBdynamic\fP\&. A pattern is considered dynamic if it cannot be used directly to find occurrences without using a matching mechanisms\&. For example, the \fR*\fP pattern is a dynamic pattern because we cannot use this pattern directly\&.
.PP

.PP
A pattern is considered dynamic if it contains the following characters (\fR…\fP — any characters or their absence) or options:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
The \fB`caseSensitiveMatch`\fP option is disabled
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\\\\\fP (the escape character)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR*\fP, \fR?\fP, \fR!\fP (at the beginning of line)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[…]\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR(…|…)\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR@(…)\fP, \fR!(…)\fP, \fR*(…)\fP, \fR?(…)\fP, \fR+(…)\fP (respects the \fB`extglob`\fP option)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR{…,…}\fP, \fR{…\&.\&.…}\fP (respects the \fB`braceExpansion`\fP option)
.PP
.SH "How to write patterns on Windows?"
.PP
Always use forward-slashes in glob expressions (patterns and \fB`ignore`\fP option)\&. Use backslashes for escaping characters\&. With the \fB`cwd`\fP option use a convenient format\&.

.PP
\fBBad\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
[
    'directory\\\\*',
    path\&.join(process\&.cwd(), '**')
]
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBGood\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
[
    'directory/*',
    fg\&.convertPathToPattern(process\&.cwd()) + '/**'
]
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:book: Use the \fB`\&.convertPathToPattern`\fP package to convert Windows-style path to a Unix-style path\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Read more about \fRmatching with backslashes\fP\&.
.SH "Why are parentheses match wrong?"
.PP
.PP
.nf
dir/
└── (special\-*file)\&.txt
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync(['(special\-*file)\&.txt']) // []
.fi
.PP

.PP
Refers to Bash\&. You need to escape special characters:

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync(['\\\\(special\-*file\\\\)\&.txt']) // ['(special\-*file)\&.txt']
.fi
.PP

.PP
Read more about \fRmatching special characters as literals\fP\&. Or use the \fB`\&.escapePath`\fP\&.
.SH "How to exclude directory from reading?"
.PP
You can use a negative pattern like this: \fR!**/node_modules\fP or \fR!**/node_modules/**\fP\&. Also you can use \fB`ignore`\fP option\&. Just look at the example below\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
first/
├── file\&.md
└── second/
    └── file\&.txt
.fi
.PP

.PP
If you don't want to read the \fRsecond\fP directory, you must write the following pattern: \fR!**/second\fP or \fR!**/second/**\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
fg\&.sync(['**/*\&.md', '!**/second']);                 // ['first/file\&.md']
fg\&.sync(['**/*\&.md'], { ignore: ['**/second/**'] }); // ['first/file\&.md']
.fi
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
:warning: When you write \fR!**/second/**/*\fP it means that the directory will be \fBread\fP, but all the entries will not be included in the results\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
You have to understand that if you write the pattern to exclude directories, then the directory will not be read under any circumstances\&.
.SH "How to use UNC path?"
.PP
You cannot use \fRUniform Naming Convention (UNC)\fP paths as patterns (due to syntax) directly, but you can use them as \fB`cwd`\fP directory or use the \fRfg\&.convertPathToPattern\fP method\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// cwd
fg\&.sync('*', { cwd: '\\\\\\\\?\\\\C:\\\\Python27' /* or //?/C:/Python27 */ });
fg\&.sync('Python27/*', { cwd: '\\\\\\\\?\\\\C:\\\\' /* or //?/C:/ */ });

// \&.convertPathToPattern
fg\&.sync(fg\&.convertPathToPattern('\\\\\\\\?\\\\c:\\\\Python27') + '/*');
.fi
.PP
.SH "Compatible with \fRnode-glob\fP?"
.PP
node-glob  fast-glob  \fRcwd\fP  \fB`cwd`\fP  \fRroot\fP  –  \fRdot\fP  \fB`dot`\fP  \fRnomount\fP  –  \fRmark\fP  \fB`markDirectories`\fP  \fRnosort\fP  –  \fRnounique\fP  \fB`unique`\fP  \fRnobrace\fP  \fB`braceExpansion`\fP  \fRnoglobstar\fP  \fB`globstar`\fP  \fRnoext\fP  \fB`extglob`\fP  \fRnocase\fP  \fB`caseSensitiveMatch`\fP  \fRmatchBase\fP  \fB`baseNameMatch`\fP  \fRnodir\fP  \fB`onlyFiles`\fP  \fRignore\fP  \fB`ignore`\fP  \fRfollow\fP  \fB`followSymbolicLinks`\fP  \fRrealpath\fP  –  \fRabsolute\fP  \fB`absolute`\fP  
